A1. What Napoleon brought with him when he came to this world and took away with him when he died. ~ NOTHING
A7. A kind of a stick that flappers use when they walk on Fifth avenue. ~ SWAGGER
A14. A man who used a whole brass band and made the people like it. ~ SOUSA
A16. What that stuff about the early bird and the worm is. (Not apple sauce.) ~ AXIOM
A18. When you hate to get up. ~ AM
A20. Where delegates get when the band wagon starts. ~ ON
A21. What all other cities are to the New Yorker. ~ BURGS
A24. Our continent (abbr.). ~ NA
A25. Frequent utterance of the amateur orator. ~ ER
A26. What you do when you look at a painting. ~ LOOK
A29. What the soldier who was caught without a pass in the captain's uniform was. (Ask any ex-service man.) ~ SOL
A31. This kind of a driver certainly takes life easily. ~ TAXI
A33. One of several things used in a racing scull. ~ OAR
A34. What the small boy did to the big apple. ~ ATE
A36. What a person is sometimes called who is always playing in hard luck. ~ WIGHT
A39. What you get when you add your assets. ~ SUM
A40. Preposition. ~ ON
A41. Envy turns them this way. ~ GREEN
A43. Something you get when your best girl turns you down. ~ BLUES
A44. Doctor of Music (abbr.). There's lots of sick music around. ~ DM
A45. A financial distress signal. ~ NSF
A47. A gentleman who could eat no fat. ~ SPRAT
A49. What you'll come to if you follow the road far enough. ~ END
A50. About the only kind of a strong drink your friends will give you nowadays. ~ WEE
A51. The god of love. ~ EROS
A53. What the stairs always do when you try to slip in. ~ CREAK
A55. An exclamation. ~ OH
A56. Where all good policemen come from. ~ ERIN
A57. A hardy, democratic, quadrennial. ~ BRYAN
A58. What he does all his life rather than beat you out of it. ~ OWES
A60. Make "special note" of this (abbr.). ~ NB
A61. Where your paper comes from. ~ PRESS
A62. Something you're told you can catch birds with. ~ SALT
A64. A possessive verb. ~ HAS
A66. Spanish for river. ~ RIO
A67. What it takes to make a football game (pl.). ~ TEAMS
A69. What you call your sister. ~ SIS
A71. What boys say when they get a licking. ~ OW
A72. What the old-time dentist does to your teeth. ~ YANKS
A74. What second-hand cars do to your pocketbook. ~ DRAIN
A75. A North American country (abbr.). ~ NS
A76. What we like to do Thanksgiving Day. ~ EAT
A78. Something American horses showed Papyrus and Epinard. ~ HEELS
A80. The two Colonels that U. S. is proud of. (Abbr.). ~ TRS
A81. It sounds like new, but it isn't. ~ GNU
A82. The Sun-maid asks you if you have had it to-day. ~ IRON
A84. What connection is used here between Damon and Pythias. ~ AND
A86. Something that has a meter. (Not a taxi.) ~ POEM
A87. A western State (abbr.). ~ ND
A88. What you give the Follies girls when you see them. ~ OO
A90. Unpleasant things you and your sweetie have. ~ SPATS
A92. What Johnny calls his mother. ~ MA
A93. Regarding. ~ RE
A94. Something that the Judge is a sure cure for. ~ ENNUI
A96. What we hope Congress won't do to any proposed Blue Laws. ~ ENACT
A99. A make of an auto. ~ STEARNS
A100. Eve was made for Adam's —?— Company. ~ EXPRESS

D2. Bone (Latin). ~ OS
D3. What the phone girl did with your message. ~ TOOK
D4. A participant in the great war. ~ HUN
D5. Part of the verb "be." ~ IS
D6. Something the police don't do to most crooks. ~ NAB
D8. Another part of the verb "be." ~ WAS
D9. What the cook gave the chicken. ~ AX
D10. What oranges were invented for. ~ GIN
D11. Don't let anyone get yours. ~ GOAT
D12. Printer's measure. ~ EM
D13. Gone from sight but not forgotten (pl.). ~ SALOONS
D15. Men have a right to do this when they win an argument with the wife. ~ CROW
D17. What tourists get in Greenwich Village. ~ TRIMMED
D19. What went up in the Democratic Party on November 15 last. ~ MOANS
D22. The best place in the world. (Ask any hundred percenter.) ~ US
D23. The kind of a tongue it takes to talk the boss into a raise. ~ GLIB
D25. Something mints won't keep that whisky flavored breath from doing. ~ EXUDE
D27. Alternative conjunction. ~ OR
D28. The hardest things to learn about dancing. ~ STEPS
D30. A short, thick piece of wood. ~ CHUNK
D32. Make it "like this." ~ AS
D34. What they ought to convict Reds for. ~ ARSON
D35. Poetical ever. ~ EER
D37. What reporters do about information. ~ GLEAN
D38. A man's nickname. ~ TED
D42. Mother of Pearl. (Not Mrs. White). ~ NACRE
D46. Plants having fronds. ~ FERNS
D48. What you would like to keep with her. ~ TRYST
D50. What a little nip does to your appetite. ~ WHETS
D52. That part of a man that has troubled him since the days of Eden. ~ RIB
D54. What your Bootlegger's acquittal did to your mind. ~ EASED
D55. Who-o-o-o is this? ~ OWL
D57. A devil of a way to be. ~ BROKE
D58. What a well filled cellar is. ~ OASIS
D59. What the Village Blacksmith used to be doing. ~ SHOEING
D61. What her sweetie does when she's away. (They are not married.) ~ PINES
D62. The way you have to be to fool your wife. ~ SMART
D63. What your interest is when the boss tells an old bromide. ~ ASSUMED
D65. What to do with a medal. ~ AWARD
D66. Something in any school yell. ~ RAH
D68. Something very hard to find in Greenwich Village. ~ ART
D70. What part puts the go in a golf ball. ~ INNER
D73. What you might get when you steal a kiss. ~ SLAP
D77. A preposition. ~ TO
D79. What part of your fingers is it that you wouldn't give for Volstead's friendship? ~ SNAP
D81. What to do when the cop tells you to move on. ~ GO
D83. The number of good deeds "Izzy and Moe" have done. ~ NONE
D85. What post prohibition gives you (abbr.). ~ DT
D86. What it is that kills nowadays according to the easy-going old-timer. ~ PACE
D89. The inside part of Jonah. ~ ONA
D90. What the Blue Law people think fun is. ~ SIN
D91. What the prudes howl about present-day shows and books. ~ SEX
D92. What that black eye does to your face. ~ MAR
D94. "À la Street Urchin" for ate. ~ ET
D95. A river in Italy. ~ UR
D97. Something you swear for (abbr.). ~ NP
D98. The way a cross-eyed man might abbreviate a thoroughfare. ~ TS


